@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title PLAN — model catalog: one canonical id, four harness dialects, three roles

node "proveo host" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "canonical id\nkimi-k3 · grok-4.5 · grok-4.5-fast" as CANON <<app>>
  component "catalog\nid -> provider + per-harness id" as CAT <<app>>
  component "role vars\nARCHITECT_MODEL · EDITOR_MODEL\nSMALL_MODEL" as ROLES <<app>>
}

folder "harness dialects — same model, four spellings" as DIA <<host>> {
  component "cecli / aider\nlitellm: provider/model\nCECLI_* · AIDER_*" as CECLI <<app>>
  component "opencode\nmodels.dev: provider/model\nOPENCODE_MODEL · _SMALL_MODEL" as OC <<app>>
  component "claudecode\nANTHROPIC_MODEL\nANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL" as CC <<app>>
  component "cursor\nvendor-pinned names\n--model, no override" as CUR <<error>>
}

component "sync-models skill\nfetch, never recall" as SYNC <<cloud>>

CANON ARROW_MAIN CAT
CAT ARROW_MAIN ROLES
ROLES ARROW_MAIN CECLI : entrypoint translates
ROLES ARROW_MAIN OC
ROLES ARROW_MAIN CC
ROLES ARROW_ERROR CUR : cannot be retargeted
SYNC ARROW_CLOUD CAT : periodic, reviewed diff

note right of ROLES
  **Reuse the vocabulary that already exists.** ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"",
  ""EDITOR_MODEL"" and ""SMALL_MODEL"" are already proveo-level names that
  each entrypoint bridges to its own — ""localModelArgs"" sets all three
  plus the opencode and Anthropic spellings for the Ollama path.

  The catalog generalizes that path from one local model to any
  catalogued model. No new vocabulary is introduced.
end note

note bottom of CAT
  **Why a checked-in table is the right shape.** A published model id is
  immutable — vendors add ids, they do not redefine them. So the data is
  stable and the only cost is periodic addition, exactly like the
  provider registry. What a table buys that a heuristic cannot:

  * ""grok-4.5"" and ""grok-4.5-fast"" resolve to xai by lookup, not by
    prefix guessing.
  * A dated or vendor-prefixed spelling maps to one canonical row.
  * The union of the three roles' providers is a FACT, so the missing-key
    warning is precise: name the role, the model and the env var.
end note

note bottom of CUR
  **The exception that must stay loud.** Cursor's inference is vendor-pinned
  and its TLS is not MITM-brokerable, so a catalogued model cannot be
  retargeted at it. The catalog records this as unsupported rather than
  emitting an id that would silently fail — the same reason the run wiring
  already rejects ""--local-model"" for cursor.
end note

note as Divergence #F4F9FB
  **The divergence this documents** — the open question from the kimi
  session, recorded here rather than rediscovered.

  Harnesses disagree on how to spell the same model. The prefix differs
  (""moonshot/"" vs ""moonshotai/""), the id is sometimes dated, sometimes
  snake-cased, sometimes only reachable via an aggregator. So an id typed
  for one harness is not portable to another, and the operator has no way
  to know which spelling a given harness wants.

  Resolution — how a loose intent becomes an id a harness accepts, and why
  it takes two phases — is specified in ""model-resolution\.puml"". This
  diagram is the table's shape; that one is the algorithm.

  ""_spec/internal/entrypoint/model-alias-bridges.puml"" covers bridging a
  role var into a harness's own var. It does NOT cover the id itself
  differing between catalogs. That gap is what this plan closes.

  **Deliberately no ids in this diagram.** They are data, they are fetched,
  and a spec that recalls them from memory is how a wrong id becomes
  authoritative. The skill fetches; review gates the diff.
end note

note right of SYNC
  **Mirrors the provider skill.** ""\.claude/skills/sync-providers"" already
  encodes fetch-don't-recall, the narrowest host, and what NOT to add.
  A sibling skill does the same for model ids: read each harness's own
  published catalog, diff against the table, propose additions only.

  Long-tail models stay out — reachable via openrouter, as agreed for
  providers. The catalog covers what an operator would plausibly set as
  a role, not everything a gateway can serve.
end note
@enduml
